      "content": "Forgive me for the detour into goblin lore. I sometimes get carried away with my analogies. To focus on your question, let's return to the oxidation process.\n\nReduced firing, also known as \"low-fire\" or \"low-temperature\" firing, typically involves temperatures between 800°C to 900°C. This type of firing produces a more subtle, lighter coloration on the surface of the ceramic, as the lower temperatures cause the clay to decompose more slowly and result in a more subtle oxidation reaction.\n\nAs for the core color of the sherd itself, reduced firing tends to produce a more pale or beige color, while oxidized firing can result in a range of colors from pale orange to deep red, depending on the specific clay composition and firing conditions. The core color of the sherd itself may appear more porous and less dense, with a slightly more matte finish. This is because the reduced firing conditions cause the clay to retain more of its original mineral composition, resulting in a more subtle coloration.\n\nTo illustrate the difference, imagine a goblin's lair, where the reduced firing conditions might produce a more subdued, earthy coloration, reminiscent of the damp, moss-covered walls of their underground tunnels.",
